  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance: duty, VAT, HS codes and the SABER/SASO differentiator</h2>

      <h3>Duty and VAT</h3>
      <p>
        Qatar's baseline import duty is <strong>5% on CIF value</strong>, with exemptions for
        essential food and some pharmaceuticals and higher rates on alcohol and tobacco. Import VAT
        is currently <strong>0%</strong>: Qatar signed the GCC VAT framework but has not implemented
        it, and an e-invoicing law passed in May 2026 signals VAT is coming — widely expected at
        ~5%. De minimis is <strong>not published</strong>; confirm with the General Authority of
        Customs.
      </p>

      <h3>SABER/SASO is Saudi-only</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>SABER/SASO is a Saudi conformity programme and does not apply to Qatar.</strong> This
        is a key differentiator: shippers who assume GCC-wide SABER is required end up chasing
        certificates they do not need. Qatar instead applies a 10-digit HS classification, import
        declaration via the General Authority of Customs, product-specific permits for regulated
        categories, and e-invoicing readiness ahead of VAT.
      </p>

      <h3>Documents and classification</h3>
      <p>
        The standard document set is the <strong>air waybill</strong>, commercial invoice, packing
        list and certificate of origin, with the correct 10-digit HS code. Classify before quoting
        because duty and any permit requirements depend on the code, and keep the air waybill
        reference ready for the clearance file.
      </p>

      <p class="small muted">
        Duty, VAT, HS classification and Hamad Port facts on this page are HIGH confidence from the
        WorldFreightHub GCC country and port data; airport roles are LOW confidence operational
        routing context.
      </p>

      <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Qatar compliance" />
  </section>

